element-transformation-2f31d517.interpretation:
The formula $^{A}_{Z}X 	o ^{A}_{Z+1}Y + e^- +  ar{
u}_e$ describes the transformation of an unstable parent nucleus, denoted by $X$, into a daughter nucleus, $Y$. The superscript $A$ represents the mass number (the total count of protons and neutrons), which remains constant during the decay ($\Delta A = 0$). The subscript $Z$ is the atomic number (the count of protons), which increases by one ($\Delta Z = +1$), indicating the transmutation of element $X$ into the next element $Y$ on the periodic table. The decay products are an electron ($e^-$) and an electron antineutrino ($ ar{
u}_e$). The emission of the antineutrino is essential for the conservation of lepton number, energy, and angular momentum.

element-transformation-2f31d517.conceptual_definition:
Beta minus ($ eta^-$) decay is a nuclear process governed by the weak interaction, where a neutron ($n$) within a parent nucleus ($^{A}_{Z}X$) is converted into a proton ($p$), transforming it into a daughter nucleus ($^{A}_{Z+1}Y$). This transmutation results in the emission of an electron ($e^-$), also known as a beta particle, and an electron antineutrino ($ ar{
u}_e$). The underlying quark-level process is the transformation of a down quark to an up quark via the emission of a virtual $W^-$ boson, which subsequently decays: $d 	o u + W^-$ and $W^- 	o e^- +  ar{
u}_e$.
